<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diego de Almagro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1475–<a href="/years/1538">1538</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diego de Almagro was a Spanish conquistador and partner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. In 1534 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> granted him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate-of-new-toledo">Governorate of New Toledo</a> south of Pizarro's lands, and he led the first Spanish expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> in 1535–1537. His claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> led to war with the Pizarros, who defeated him at Las Salinas in 1538 and had him executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro.jpg" alt="Diego de Almagro" width="320" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Domingo Mesa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diego_de_Almagro.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Diego de Almagro was born of humble origins, the illegitimate son of Juan de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and Elvira Gutiérrez, in the village of Almagro or in Malagón in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudad-real">Ciudad Real</a>, and took his surname from the village. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> he served in the household of Luis Gonzalez de Polanco, who used his influence to secure him a passage to the New World after Almagro stabbed another servant.</p><p>He landed at Santa María la Antigua del Darién, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, on 30 June 1514, with the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedrarias-davila">Pedro Arias Dávila</a>. There he received an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> and farmed, and on an expedition under Gaspar de Espinosa he became a close friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and Hernando de Luque. By 1524 the three had formed an association to conquer lands to the south. He lost an eye to an arrow at Punta Quemada.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Pizarro's defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a> in 1532, Almagro joined him with more men and arms. In August 1534 he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> near Lake Colta, to forestall Sebastián de Belalcázar, and in December of that year Trujillo, which he named after Pizarro's birthplace.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> divided the conquered lands between Nueva Castilla, for Pizarro, and Nueva Toledo, for Almagro. Almagro left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> on 3 July 1535 with about 750 Spaniards, following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> trail and then crossing the Andes, where cold and hunger killed Spaniards and, above all slaves unused to the climate. He took possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> in the Copiapó valley and explored as far as the Aconcagua. Almagro found neither gold nor cities, and his troops plundered local property and carried off captives as porters and slaves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Almagro returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> in 1537. On the night of 8 April 1537 his men seized the city and imprisoned Hernando and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a>, and on 12 July 1537 they defeated an army sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> at the Battle of Abancay. Hernando was freed in return for control of Cuzco. In April 1538 the Almagristas were beaten at Las Salinas and Rodrigo Orgóñez was killed. Almagro fled to Cuzco but was captured, and his appeal to the king was ignored.</p><p>He was garrotted in his dungeon and beheaded on 8 July 1538. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro-the-younger">Diego de Almagro II</a>, called El Mozo, was proclaimed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> by the conspirators who killed Pizarro on 26 June 1541. He was defeated at Chupas on 16 September 1542 and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diego de Almagro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In January 1537 Manco repulsed a Spanish attack at Ollantaytambo by flooding the plain below its terraces, but the return of <b>Diego de Almagro</b>’s army from Chile in April made the Sacred Valley untenable.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…divided the conquered lands into the governorates of New Castile, granted to Pizarro, and New Toledo, granted to <b>Diego de Almagro</b>, but rivalry between the two conquerors led to civil war, the execution of Almagro in 1538 and the assassination of Pizarro in 1541 in Lima, the city…</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Diego de Almagro</b>’s expedition was turned back after the Battle of Reynogüelén in 1536, and Pedro de Valdivia founded Santiago in 1541, where the northern Mapuche leader Michimalonco, who had recently defeated the Inca governor, attacked and destroyed the town on 11 September 1541.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mapuche">Mapuche · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishop was sailing to Peru to settle a dispute between Francisco Pizarro and <b>Diego de Almagro</b> when the winds died and strong currents carried his ship out to the Galápagos Islands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-10">10 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The boundary with <b>Diego de Almagro's</b> Governorate of New Toledo, especially the possession of Cusco, led to civil war, and Almagro was executed in 1538.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-new-castile">Governorate of New Castile</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1534 Charles V granted <b>Diego de Almagro</b>, Francisco Pizarro's partner in the conquest of Peru, a governorate of 200 leagues south of the Governorate of New Castile.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-new-toledo">Governorate of New Toledo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Almagro, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.89,-3.71&amp;year=1515" aria-label="Almagro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Diego de Almagro"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4753 4239h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4753 4239h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.89,-3.71&amp;year=1515">What was here in 1515? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
